Z||000000||CUTTER||08-26-99||01:48 AM||||I usually put 91 or 94 octane in with a  octane boost I can buy at a K-mart for under 5 bucks, does it make much of a difference in perpormane? I think I can feel the difference...but I think my mind is making me feel the difference even though there is none...my mind wants me too think there is. umm..anyways is this high of octane hurt your engine? how many horsepower increase if any? regular gas better? this is something I think alot of people screw off....the gas...they want to save a few cents and put in a cheaper grade...but I need all the horsepower I can get and put in 94 octane and a octane boost.<br>sorry this is so complicated and long
Z||000001||batboy||08-26-99||06:02 AM||||Unless you're running some very high compression pistons (more than 8.9:1) the octane booster is probably unneccessary if you are already using premium grade gasoline. 
Z||000002||Formula||08-26-99||10:08 AM||||the only thing you need that high of octane in the 2.8 v6 is if it is pinging. or if you have a chip.
Z||000003||mwbackus||08-26-99||03:31 PM||||You really will see very little performance benefit from "extremely" high octane levels as far as the stock V6 is concerned.  But many have mods such as chips and turbos that would benefit.  Here is how to make your own octane booster (this is the basic formula of one of the popular octane booster products). <p>100 oz of toulene<br>25 oz of mineral spirits<br>3 oz of transmission fluid<p>This will give you 8 16oz bottles.  You can get a whole gallon of toulene for less than $3.00.<p>Mike<br>
Z||000004||Cooter||08-26-99||09:03 PM||||Or just go to the hobby shop and pick up the gallon of 4cyl airplane fuel with 45% nitromethane. The rest of the fuel is methanol and castor oil. I used a blend of this while racing "stock" gas class gokarts. It would really wake up a roto-tiller motor!  It smells really neat too. Just had a problem with burned pistons. I dont know why. . .
Z||000005||Patrick||08-30-99||05:18 PM||||<br>Mike, I'm intrigued by your mixture. Is there any harm to be done using this stuff? How much is needed, one 16oz bottle per Fiero tankful or what?<p>Which would be cheaper, adding this to regular gas, or simply buying premium?<p>I'm surprised that no-one else has commented on this "potion".<p> 
Z||000006||theogre||08-30-99||07:56 PM||||the nitro probly won't hurt the engine or cat, but the oil might mess with the cat if you use to much.
Z||000007||mwbackus||08-30-99||10:31 PM||||Patrick:  The formula is pretty basic as far as octane boosters go. Gasoline is little more than a mixture of various hydrocarbons which includes toulene as a constiuent, so it would be safe to assume that "conservative" levels would do no harm to your motor.<br>    Other naptha hydrocarbons such as Xylene, Methyl-tertiary-butyl-ether, and even Benzene (Note: Benzene is a carcinogen), are excellent octane boosters.  A 16 oz. bottle of the formula I listed will boost a tank of premium fuel about 3 to 4 points (ie..from 92 to 92.3).  Since regular fuel contains insufficient amounts of the additives which promote the complete burning of higher octane components, it is best to use these boosters with at least the median grade (between reg. and prem.) fuel.  I would HIGHLY discourage anyone from adding Nitromethane to their fuel unless they know "exactly" what they are doing.  A little too much will give detonation a whole new meaning. :-)<p><br>Mike <br>ACS Certified Analytical Chemist<br>Explosives research scientist - retired.
Z||000008||Racer 1||08-30-99||11:24 PM||||Go to your local airport and fill up with aviation fuel. Its over 100 octane.My friend used it in his dirtbike ran ok. I cant use  my local airport fuel because its a AFB I might get shot by the MP's going out on the flight line.<br>Home of the stealth fighters based here at Holloman AFB...
Z||000009||lowCG||08-31-99||12:06 AM||||AV gas is of a different molecular weight,and will require a different(mixture) fuel curve;stick with the pump(automotive)variety,unless you own an airport,and like to mess around alot with fuel injection systems.<br>As for octane booster,combustion chamber characteristics will determine need,and usually any motor will seem to run better with an octane boost,whether it's really needed or not depends on the tune(timing,mixture sttings)and yes,it will gain horsepower,if the settings are correct for the fuel,and the compression ratio is high enough,less than 9:1 shouldn't really need premium.I've found that running regular gas in a four cylinder Fiero will ping all over,and that it's much easier to tune with premium,however.<br>Haven't tried much with the(Fiero)sixes,but my Dad's Celebrity wagon doesn't care what kind of fuel it gets.
Z||000010||batboy||08-31-99||06:11 AM||||I would not use straight aviation fuel, it's a different density etc. and could evenually cause engine damage. Back when I was racing my Chevelle, I'd put a couple gallons of aviation fuel into a tank of the best premium pump gas I could find for a octane boost.
Z||000011||lowCG||08-31-99||12:36 PM||||Forgot to mention it,but there's Trik gas,available at limited locations,maybe near you.This stuff is awsome in a car with some compression,smells neat too.Was leaving the pumps in a '68 Camaro with a pumped up small block,and all of a sudden the exhaust note changed as the fuel got to the motor.
Z||000012||Greg Piet||08-31-99||01:41 PM||||I found this site while answering another fuel question on here.<br>If you are that concerned with high octane fuel, try fuel designed for racing <br>http://vpfuels.com/fuel2.html
Z||000013||DXR_WILL||08-31-99||05:55 PM||||Here in California 76 Gasoline stations have 100 Octane high performance gasoline.  My uncle uses it for his 70' Camaro Z-28, and he says his car runs a lot smoother.  Has anyone tried using it before?  Out here it's like 4 bucks a gallon.  But I'm pretty sure it's a lot cheaper in any other state, being that us Californians have the hight gas prices, I think.
Z||000014||theogre||08-31-99||08:21 PM||||Well, If I understand it right.....<p>Adding octain numbers to fuel won't improve power output. It will reduce knocking if you've upped the compression, or have a bit of carbon.<p>Higher octain pump mixes are also more volitile. This increased volitility would increase power to a point, but they also have to boost octain numbers or it pings easier. So at the pump the 2 go together.<p>Adding a product to a pump mix that doesn't increase volitility doesn't boost power.<p>Having tried octain boosters in 89-92 pump gas a few times, I have to say I didn't see any power boost. Even from the 104+ stuff.
Z||000015||Dale||09-01-99||03:00 AM||||If I search my memory (which doesn't always work) the higher the octane the slower the burn which translates into a more complete burn.  This is one of the reasons water injection helps.
